{"id": "c7214d52-f8a5-413e-a9ad-e7a4e0dbc19e", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I've got the full methodology loaded. Now let me pull the actual infrastructure data for the `telemetry-pipeline` stack in eu-west-1 — this involves checking many AWS layers (compute, data stores, networking, backups, etc.), so I'll run that as a background research pass and bring back just the findings.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_woCEO0GoNHSeQDZBSEV0Ex", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"deep_research": true, "prompt": "{
  "task": "Gather read-only resilience data for the 'telemetry-pipeline' CloudFormation stack in eu-west-1 (AWS account 012345678901_1), to assess survivability of an Availability Zone (AZ) failure and a Region failure. This is for a resilience review, not an incident investigation.",
  "context": "User ingests device telemetry via this stack in eu-west-1 and wants to know what happens to it on an AZ failure or a Region failure, and what to fix first. We need an inventory of every component layer by layer, with its current redundancy/backup/cross-Region posture, so a resilience report can be written from it afterward. Return DATA ONLY (no prose recommendations) as structured rows.",
  "method": "1) First check if 'telemetry-pipeline' (or a close name) already exists as a service in the next-generation AWS Resilience Hub (resiliencehubv2) in eu-west-1 — list services, and if found, get its resilience policy (RTO/RPO), its resources, and any open failure mode findings. 2) Call cloudformation ListStacks/DescribeStacks and ListStackResources for the stack named 'telemetry-pipeline' in eu-west-1. If no exact match exists, list the closest candidate stack names and stop (do not substitute). 3) For every resource in the stack, group into layers (entry/routing, compute, data, storage, messaging/events, identity, shared dependencies) and check: for compute (EC2/ASG/ECS/EKS/Lambda/Step Functions) - how many AZs/subnets it spans, single-task or single-instance risk, where AMIs/images/code come from and whether they exist outside eu-west-1; for data stores (DynamoDB/RDS/Aurora/ElastiCache/Kinesis/OpenSearch etc.) - Multi-AZ status, point-in-time-recovery/continuous backups status, on-demand backups, whether any replica/global-table/cross-Region copy exists; for messaging (Kinesis/SQS/SNS/MSK/EventBridge) - dead-letter queue presence, retention, whether equivalents exist in another Region; for storage (S3/EFS/EBS) - versioning, replication configuration, snapshot copies outside the Region; for entry/routing (ALB/NLB/API Gateway/CloudFront/Route 53) - AZ span, failover routing configuration; for shared dependencies - Secrets Manager/KMS key replication, VPC NAT gateway topology (one zonal NAT per AZ vs single NAT), service quotas. 4) Check AWS Backup: ListBackupPlans, ListProtectedResources, ListRecoveryPointsByResource for the stack's resources, and DescribeRegionSettings for DynamoDB advanced features if DynamoDB is present. 5) Note any call that is denied or returns empty, label as 'not checked' rather than 'no gap'.",
  "resources": ["CloudFormation stack: telemetry-pipeline", "region: eu-west-1", "account: 012345678901_1"],
  "timeframe": "current state (not historical)",
  "criteria": "Return one row per resource in this exact shape: layer | resource | type | Region | in-Region redundancy (AZs, nodes, tasks) | backups (kind, frequency, retention) | copies, images or IaC outside the Region | point-in-time recovery | replication | what could not be read. Also separately report: (a) whether this stack is already a Resilience Hub service, and if so its policy RTO/RPO and open findings; (b) any resources connected to the stack by configuration (e.g., a DynamoDB table or Kinesis stream the stack's Lambda writes to) that are NOT literal stack resources — list them as possible members. Do not give recommendations or a roadmap — that will be written separately from this data."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Survey telemetry-pipeline CloudFormation stack in eu-west-1 for AZ/Region failure resilience"}], "created_at": null}
